#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175725 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41472e46-65d2-579b-85b7-309749ed3a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "2ed3f2e8-2d0e-59e0-a9dc-1856245b768c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1343" :question/problem "Factor $3x^2 - 21x + 30.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-21x+30 & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-7x+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2-7x+10$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(10),$ but only the negative factor pairs since the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is negative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1,-10),\\,(-2,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-10) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-5) & =-7 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-7x=-2x-5x,$ and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-5x+10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & = \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-5)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3\\left(x^2-7x+10\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n3(x-5)(x-2)\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term"}] :question/id #uuid "1c852599-e08e-5b54-be2b-700c3f8f47f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6931" :question/problem "Factor the expression $-2x^2-22x-48.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-2$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-22x-48 & = \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+11x+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2+11x+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,24),\\,(2,12),\\,(3,8),\\,(4,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(11)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+24 & =25 & \\times  \\\\ 2+12 & =14 & \\times  \\\\ 3+8 & =11 & ✓ \\\\ 4+6 & =10 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $11x=3x+8x,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n-2\\left(x^2+11x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left(x^2+3x+8x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left((x^2+3x)+(8x+24)\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\Bigl(x(x+3)+8(x+3)\\Bigr)&=\\[5pt]\n-2(x+8)(x+3)\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41472e46-65d2-579b-85b7-309749ed3a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492942ee-22f4-59db-934a-e22733b6184d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5t-4)(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499791} {:answer/id #uuid "52b70ec8-bf60-5ca2-9500-bf2667045ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499811} {:answer/id #uuid "9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(t-4)(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499955} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e27368-2343-5732-958d-8a429ac47df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500034} {:answer/id #uuid "ef378e59-9ad9-542b-aa16-7399d5fbff76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(t+4)(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(t-4)(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499955} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c06265-2366-51a7-b0d0-03e55f741385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51a9e6b4-a8d3-5a57-a795-b4c3e7575128" :question/math-academy-id "q-2855" :question/problem "$5{t}^{2}-15t-20=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2}-15t-20 & =5({t}^{2}-3t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${t}^{2}-3t-4$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-4).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-4) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 4 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-4) & =-3 & ✓ \\\\ (-1)+4 & =3 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-2) & =0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3t=t-4t$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t-4 & = \\\\ {t}^{2}+t-4t-4 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+t)+(-4t-4) & = \\\\ t(t+1)-4(t+1) & = \\\\ (t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =5(t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a52351-b0ea-5214-b447-eb8bfc25cede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174919} {:answer/id #uuid "c44caa58-b68e-5fc5-ae37-46ffc70f9415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} {:answer/id #uuid "e5830896-5bea-546f-8950-0461dcc18f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} {:answer/id #uuid "e88abefb-6bda-5f3c-9741-77dfe56290a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174922} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddace94-63fa-5071-994a-652bc14cb466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44caa58-b68e-5fc5-ae37-46ffc70f9415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} :answer-field/id #uuid "e57dad43-30f5-5af5-b15c-170042285046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c398936-3982-5ba0-b353-c27acab63b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-32105" :question/problem "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41472e46-65d2-579b-85b7-309749ed3a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b107508-c497-5022-876e-eecbf2e11f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499830} {:answer/id #uuid "7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499894} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdcfe25-e05c-5e4e-b69a-8716e49777a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499956} {:answer/id #uuid "a936416f-f189-5f46-bd0f-f7f48beaf553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499984} {:answer/id #uuid "d3062eb8-1be4-55c1-abe5-0531007f28e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499894} :answer-field/id #uuid "06764b2b-0fcb-5201-9043-354b15b5afa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7598e646-e70e-5042-8a78-36ec6c081791" :question/math-academy-id "q-32107" :question/problem "If $5{b}^{2}-15b+10=5(b+p)(b+q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{2}-15b+10 & =5({b}^{2}-3b+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${b}^{2}-3b+2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(2).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-2) & =-3 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3b=-b-2b$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-3b+2 & = \\\\ {b}^{2}-b-2b+2 & = \\\\ ({b}^{2}-b)+(-2b+2) & = \\\\ b(b-1)-2(b-1) & = \\\\ (b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({b}^{2}-3b+2) & =5(b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-2,q=-1,$ and $p+q=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(x+2)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499659} {:answer/id #uuid "7be4e3a3-34cb-572b-8974-2b87a20cfbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499886} {:answer/id #uuid "9c579a24-2a9d-597c-bcee-586175e4ecd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499957} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8abbbd-95f4-5109-a120-3ff6ea915413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500003} {:answer/id #uuid "b601254e-067f-525c-b190-c1e96f078bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(x+2)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499659} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ecc6ff-8871-56b2-aab9-81dfb39a048c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8115771-d0c0-58ad-81dd-b9545f4b8d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-80173" :question/problem "Factor $6-3x-3{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $(-3)$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3x-3{x}^{2} & =-3({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${x}^{2}+x-2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(-2).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-2) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-2) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ (-1)+(2) & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $x=-x+2x$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ -3({x}^{2}-x+2x-2) & = \\\\ -3(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ -3(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ -3(x+2)(x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
